#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049077 :topic/id #uuid "d8c4ac32-4132-52a4-ae3a-c84da7590f1f" :topic/knowledge-points [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087704 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087441 :question/id #uuid "ce7cee67-006d-58f3-b0df-7dd305d698fe" :question/math-academy-id "e-14803" :question/problem "What is the coefficient of the quadratic term in the equation $-5x^2+3x+7=0$?" :question/worked-solution "The quadratic term is $-5x^2$ and its coefficient is $-5$."} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3a31f0e9-83ad-5e69-a23c-14d64209dfb5" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Terms of a Quadratic Equation"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087705 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087442 :question/id #uuid "267f4395-33dd-5f8a-9075-d8240096266e" :question/math-academy-id "e-14806" :question/problem "Is $2x^2+3-x=x^2$ a quadratic equation?" :question/worked-solution "Let's try to write our equation in the standard form of a quadratic equation,\n\n$$\nax^2 + bx + c = 0\n$$\n\nwhere $a$, $b$, and $c$ are constants and\n\n$$\na \\neq 0\n$$\n\nFirst, we can collect the $x^2$ terms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n2x^{2} + 3 - x &= x^{2} \\\\\n2x^{2} + 3 - x - x^{2} &= x^{2} - x^{2} \\\\\n(2x^{2} - x^{2}) + 3 - x &= 0 \\\\\nx^{2} + 3 - x &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, let's put the terms in order:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx^{2} + 3 - x &= 0 \\\\\nx^{2} - x + 3 &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe see that our equation is in the standard form of a quadratic equation with $a=1$, $b=-1$, and $c=3$.\n\nTherefore, our equation is a quadratic equation."} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "39a573a9-ee8e-5a11-a48e-872de4ec71ee" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Quadratic Equations"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087706 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087443 :question/id #uuid "a3223541-08f5-5782-bd3d-c064cf0a9f65" :question/math-academy-id "e-14810" :question/problem "Write the quadratic equation $(y+1)(3y+2) = y^2$ in standard form." :question/worked-solution "The standard form of a quadratic equation is\n\n$$\nax^2 + bx + c = 0\n$$\n\nwhere $a$, $b$, and $c$ are constants and\n\n$$\na \\neq 0\n$$\n\nThe variable is $y$ here, but the same idea still applies. To write this equation in standard form, we start by expanding the binomial product on the left-hand side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(y + 1)(3y + 2) &= y^{2} \\\\\ny(3y + 2) + 1(3y + 2) &= y^{2} \\\\\n3y^{2} + 2y + 3y + 2 &= y^{2} \\\\\n3y^{2} + 5y + 2 &= y^{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we collect the $y^2$ terms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n3y^{2} + 5y + 2 &= y^{2} \\\\\n3y^{2} + 5y + 2 - y^{2} &= y^{2} - y^{2} \\\\\n(3y^{2} - y^{2}) + 5y + 2 &= 0 \\\\\n2y^{2} + 5y + 2 &= 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe see that our equation is in the standard form of a quadratic equation with $a=2$, $b=5$, and $c=2$.\n\nTherefore, our equation is a quadratic equation."} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "af38f6e8-af5e-5964-954b-b721b05c6380" :knowledge-point/title "Writing a Quadratic Equation in Standard Form"}] :topic/math-academy-id 813 :topic/next [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048963} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049724} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050296} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050465} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050845}] :topic/title "Introduction to Quadratic Equations"}]}
